+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md - Why. The relevant current state:
+
+- `src/app.ts` wires `OrderService(orderRepo, clock)` and `CouponService(couponRepo)`; the two
+  domains never meet today.
+- `CouponService` already owns coupon lookups, but its `get()` raises HTTP 404 for a missing
+  coupon, while the acceptance criteria for redemption require HTTP 400 for an unusable one.
+- The database schema already carries every field this change needs: `orders.discount_cents`,
+  `orders.final_amount_cents`, `orders.coupon_code`, `coupons.max_discount_cents`,
+  `coupons.used_count` and the `coupon_redemptions` table.
+- House conventions: money is integer cents, time comes from the injected `Clock`, all SQL
+  lives in repositories, business rules live in services, errors are `AppError` with an
+  UPPER_SNAKE code.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Validate coupon eligibility and compute the discount in one place, independently of HTTP.
+- Persist the outcome atomically enough that the order and the coupon stay consistent.
+- Keep the HTTP layer thin: parse the path/body, delegate, map errors.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Enforcing `usage_limit` (the counter is maintained but not used as a gate).
+- Removing or reversing an applied coupon, or any change to the order lifecycle.
+- Authentication/authorization and concurrency control across processes.
+
+## Decisions
+
+**Coupon rules live in `CouponService`, order mutation lives in `OrderService`.**
+Add `CouponService.redeem(code, amountCents, now)` returning the coupon plus the computed
+discount; it throws `AppError(400, ...)` for a missing, disabled, out-of-window or
+under-minimum coupon. `OrderService.applyCoupon(id, couponCode)` looks up the order (404 if
+unknown), asks `CouponService` to redeem, then persists. This keeps coupon policy in one class
+and lets `get()` keep its existing 404 behavior for reads.
+
+Alternative considered: putting the whole flow in the route handler, or duplicating rules in
+`OrderService`. Rejected because it spreads coupon policy across layers.
+
+**`OrderService` gains a `CouponService` dependency.**
+It is the orchestrator for order mutations, so it takes `couponService` as a third constructor
+argument; `app.ts` updates accordingly. Existing constructors are only built in `app.ts`, so
+there is no wider ripple.
+
+**Distinct 400 error codes per rule.**
+`INVALID_REQUEST` (missing code), `COUPON_NOT_FOUND`, `COUPON_NOT_ACTIVE`, `COUPON_EXPIRED`,
+`COUPON_NOT_YET_VALID`, `COUPON_MIN_AMOUNT_NOT_MET`. All are HTTP 400 because the acceptance
+criteria treat every unusable coupon as an invalid request. Distinct codes keep the failure
+reason observable.
+
+**Discount arithmetic, integer cents only.**
+`FIXED`: `discount = min(coupon.value, orderAmountCents)`. `PERCENT`:
+`discount = floor(orderAmountCents * value / 100)`, then capped by `maxDiscountCents` when it
+is not `NULL`, then capped at `orderAmountCents`. The discount is always computed from
+`orders.amount_cents` (the original amount), so re-applying a coupon is idempotent rather than
+compounding.
+
+Alternative considered: computing percent discounts on the remaining final amount. Rejected:
+it makes results order-dependent and hard to reason about for re-application.
+
+**Persistence as one repository write plus two side-effect writes.**
+`OrderRepository.updatePricing(id, {discountCents, finalAmountCents, couponCode})` rewrites the
+order; `CouponRepository.recordRedemption(orderId, code, discountCents, createdAt)` inserts the
+redemption row and increments `used_count`. All three run inside a `BEGIN`/`COMMIT` guarded by
+`try`/`catch` in `OrderService` so a failure rolls back. SQLite via `node:sqlite` is synchronous,
+so no async plumbing is needed.
+
+**Response body is the updated order.**
+Consistent with `POST /orders/:id/pay` and `/cancel`.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [The applied coupon could become invalid later (e.g. disabled)] → Out of scope; the order
+  keeps the recorded pricing until explicitly re-priced.
+- [Rounding choice for PERCENT (floor) is not stated in the requirements] → Floor is the
+  conventional rounding for discounts in cents; it is covered by a scenario so it is visible
+  and changeable.
+- [Re-applying a coupon increments `used_count` again] → Acceptable for an auditable
+  redemption log; documented so it is not mistaken for a bug.
+- [Missing `usage_limit` enforcement] → Explicit non-goal; the counter is still maintained, so
+  enforcement can be added later without a schema change.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+No schema or data migration: the columns and tables already exist. Deploying is a code change
+only; rollback is reverting the code, since no existing rows are rewritten by the new path
+except those the new endpoint touches.
